# Pull requests

Template SSOT: `.github/pull_request_template.md` (or the chosen host's equivalent). Every section stays — fill for this branch; never leave `[placeholders]`.

## When

After the user asks to **push** (or create/update a PR/MR), in the same turn: ensure the branch is on the remote, open or refresh the PR/MR body from the template using the git host's own CLI/UI (concrete mechanics: `docs/environment/github.md`, or that host's equivalent once written), and return the PR/MR URL.

Do **not** invent a short Summary-only body. Do **not** skip Dev test / Deployment / Reversion when the template has those headings.

## How to fill

Read the template file, then rewrite each section from the real diff:

| Section | Source |
|---|---|
| **Summary** | Why + what (1–2 sentences) |
| **Scope** | Layers, routes, migrations, package pins, compat, infra-config touch |
| **Dev test plan** | Concrete local migrate/seed, URLs, SQL, user path; staging e2e URL when deployable |
| **Reviewer checklist** | Leave boxes; tick only what this PR already satisfies in the diff |
| **Infra / deploy config** | N/A or the matching boxes + plan-artifact note — see `docs/DEPLOYMENT_STRATEGY.md` |
| **Deployment plan** | Analyse → Test → Deploy staging → Migrate (if any) → e2e → merge → prod |
| **Reversion plan** | Code rollback choice; additive vs recovery SQL / restore point |

Prefer checkboxes the template already uses. Mark N/A explicitly when a section does not apply.

## Out of scope

- Do not open a PR unless the user asked to push, create a PR, or update the PR overview.
- Do not push without an explicit push/PR request.
- Commit message style stays separate; PR body is the template, not the commit subject alone.
- Merge to the integration branch only when the predeploy gate is green. Never merge while it is red. See `predeploy.mdc`.
